# An interval from two canonical 2-limb values (milestone2.md, "Interval kernels", "Values of an
# interval"): the listing of interval_make_1 on the comparisons of two limbs, le_2_2 and lt_2_2 for
# the order of the bounds and the leading limbs for the tests against infinity (a canonical NaN is
# all NaN, and a comparison with it is false). The values are limbs of objects, already canonical,
# so nothing is guarded: the library calls it on bounds make_2 wrote (the interval of a point of T,
# from_bounds of two values of T); the constructors of bounds of two limbs the program gives call
# interval_make_2_entry, this listing on the limbs guarded. The whole line when a bound is a NaN, whatever the other bound is, and when
# both bounds are the same infinity (decided on 2026-11-20, as for interval_make_1); otherwise the
# empty set when lo > hi (rv); otherwise [lo, hi], an infinite bound returned as (+-inf, 0). For
# every precision.
kernel:        interval_make_2
input:         lo = lo0 lo1
input:         hi = hi0 hi1
output:        zl = zl0 zl1
output:        zu = zu0 zu1
requires:      canonical lo
requires:      canonical hi
ensures:       makes_interval zl zu from=lo,hi
min_precision: 1
source:        constructor of an interval of two limbs (Milestone 2 listing; equal infinities the whole line, 2026-11-20)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeIntervalExact.v interval_make_2_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   every output is selected after explicit tests of the bounds; makes_interval states what comes out

ok  = le_2_2(lo0, lo1, hi0, hi1)
nl  = lt(lo0, const inf)
mi  = neg(const inf)
nu  = lt(mi, hi0)
va  = and(ok, nl)
v   = and(va, nu)
rv  = lt_2_2(hi0, hi1, lo0, lo1)
el  = select(rv, const inf, mi)
eu  = select(rv, mi, const inf)
zl0 = select(v, lo0, el)
zl1 = select(v, lo1, 0)
zu0 = select(v, hi0, eu)
zu1 = select(v, hi1, 0)
